["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Big animals leave big sign. Learn their food, their water and their marks, and you can tell from one walk whether a moose, an elk or a bear lives there.</p></aside><ul><li>This is a photo walk: moose country, elk country, bear food, then the tracks and sign of each.</li><li>Moose: willow swamps, lake edges, beaver ponds, burns and cut blocks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. The Bonaparte Plateau north of Kamloops is a provincial moose study area <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Elk: few near our bases; about 1,350 in the Thompson Okanagan area in 2000 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Roosevelt elk were released near Chehalis, not far from Mission <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Black bears: everywhere near our bases, following the food from spring greens to fall berries and fish <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Carry bear spray on every walk in this country (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-moose-willows.jpg\" alt=\"Cow moose browsing willows in winter. Twig ends bitten off at chest height.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow moose browsing willows in winter. Twig ends bitten off at chest height. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park from Yellowstone NP, USA, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_moose_browsing_on_willows_(15951276174).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-willow-shrub.jpg\" alt=\"Willow shrub in a wet mountain meadow. Long narrow leaves, many stems.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Willow shrub in a wet mountain meadow. Long narrow leaves, many stems. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salix_bebbiana_(5002370825).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-dogwood-stems.jpg\" alt=\"Red osier dogwood. Red stems, a top moose browse in winter.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Red osier dogwood. Red stems, a top moose browse in winter. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cornus_sericea_-_red_osier_dogwood_-_53066633106.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-beaver-lodge.jpg\" alt=\"Beaver pond and lodge. Beaver ponds grow the willow and water plants moose need.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Beaver pond and lodge. Beaver ponds grow the willow and water plants moose need. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Greenbilly, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beaver_pond_with_duck.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Winter moose browse: red osier dogwood, cottonwood, birch, aspen, willow and subalpine fir <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>On plateaus, summer moose spread out near lake shores, swamps and beaver ponds; in winter they gather in river valleys, burns, logged areas and wetland complexes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Moose move easily in snow up to 40 cm deep and often winter where the snow is 50 cm or more <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Willow: long narrow leaves, grows in wet ground and cut blocks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Look for broken twig tops up to 2 m high (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-moose-tracks-mud.jpg\" alt=\"Moose tracks in mud. Much bigger than deer, with long pointed toes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Moose tracks in mud. Much bigger than deer, with long pointed toes. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_Tracks_on_Seedskadee_National_Wildlife_Refuge_(22388750875).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-moose-trail-snow.jpg\" alt=\"A moose trail ploughed through deep snow among small spruce.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A moose trail ploughed through deep snow among small spruce. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS staff, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_Browse_Collection,_Yukon-Charley_Rivers,_2003_2_(1482c7be-3b1f-42da-844e-544383bbd5da).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>A moose track is about 13 to 15 cm long, more than twice a deer track <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Moose step over deep snow and logs; the trail is a trough with deep holes, not a line of prints (Tip).</li></ul><ul><li>Moose pellets are big, oval, about 2.5 to 3 cm long, and piles are large <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Fresh tracks at a beaver pond edge at dawn: sit there the next evening (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-elk-wallow.jpg\" alt=\"Elk wallow: a muddy pit in a wet meadow, dug and rolled in by bulls in the rut.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Elk wallow: a muddy pit in a wet meadow, dug and rolled in by bulls in the rut. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Loren Lane ( MORA ), Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elk_wallow._101981._slide_(f74312c976714e5db311641d02f847ac).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-elk-rub.jpg\" alt=\"Subalpine fir rubbed raw by a bull elk. Bark stripped high up the trunk.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Subalpine fir rubbed raw by a bull elk. Bark stripped high up the trunk. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Loren Lane ( MORA ), Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elk-rubbed_subalpine_fir._101981._slide_(6816ef0ab947413f8a22106e2d066c28).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-elk-tracks-shore.jpg\" alt=\"Elk tracks churning a lake shore where a herd came to drink.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Elk tracks churning a lake shore where a herd came to drink. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Loren Lane ( MORA ), Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Many_elk_tracks_at_Clover_Lake_shore._101981._slide_(e7be1efbc1e74b41b6fce2ac2b030afb).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-elk-pellets.jpg\" alt=\"Elk pellets in a meadow. Bigger than deer pellets, with a boot for scale.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Elk pellets in a meadow. Bigger than deer pellets, with a boot for scale. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Una Smith, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elk_pellets.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-elk-trail-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Elk trail in spring snow: many animals walking in single file.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Elk trail in spring snow: many animals walking in single file. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Loren Lane ( MORA ), Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elk_tracks_in_snow._111984._slide_(ba1503e681a9402f97f934fbd6d8a5d8).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>The elk rut is mid September; most cows are bred mid to late September <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Bull elk dig and roll in wallows and rub trees with their antlers in the rut <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Fresh mud and bright wood mean a bull is near (Tip).</li></ul><ul><li>Elk tracks are about 9 to 11 cm long, rounder than a deer track <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Interior elk spend summer in subalpine basins and avalanche tracks and move down with autumn snow <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-huckleberry-berry.jpg\" alt=\"Black huckleberry. Bears fatten on these in August and September.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black huckleberry. Bears fatten on these in August and September. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: julie from Idaho, united states, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaccinium_membranaceum_Fruit_and_leaves.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-soopolallie.jpg\" alt=\"Soopolallie (soapberry) with red berries, North Thompson River Park.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Soopolallie (soapberry) with red berries, North Thompson River Park. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Robert Flogaus-Faust, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shepherdia_canadensis_2_RF.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-skunk-cabbage.jpg\" alt=\"Skunk cabbage in a wet spring forest near Aldergrove. Early spring bear food.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Skunk cabbage in a wet spring forest near Aldergrove. Early spring bear food. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: The Cosmonaut, CC BY-SA 2.5 CA, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lysichiton_americanus_-_Aldergrove_Regional_Park.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bear-berry-patch.jpg\" alt=\"Black bear standing in a fall berry patch. Bears follow the ripening fruit.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black bear standing in a fall berry patch. Bears follow the ripening fruit. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: U. S. Fish and Wildlife Service - Northeast Region, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Bear_Moosehorn_National_Wildlife_Refuge_(51497306948).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Spring bear food: meadows, estuaries, stream sides, skunk cabbage, avalanche chutes, grassy south slopes and burns <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Fall bear food: berries and spawning fish <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Soopolallie berries ripen in July and August; huckleberries in August and September <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Huckleberry: shrub to about 1.5 m, thin oval leaves, dark purple berries, in mountain forest and old burns <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Source: E Flora BC.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bear-track-front.jpg\" alt=\"Black bear front track in the Pasayten, next to the Okanagan. Wide pad, 5 toes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black bear front track in the Pasayten, next to the Okanagan. Wide pad, 5 toes. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: vhhammer, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_bear_(Ursus_americanus)_track_at_the_Hidden_Lakes_of_the_Pasayten_Wilderness..jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bear-scat-okanagan.jpg\" alt=\"Black bear scat on a trail in Mission Park, Kelowna. Full of seeds and skins.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black bear scat on a trail in Mission Park, Kelowna. Full of seeds and skins. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Accuruss, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bear_Scat_Okanagan_Black_Bear.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bear-scat-berries.jpg\" alt=\"Black bear scat full of berries, with a pen for scale.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black bear scat full of berries, with a pen for scale. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWSmidwest, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Bear_Scat_(15274318020).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bear-claw-aspen.jpg\" alt=\"Claw marks on an aspen trunk where a bear climbed.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Claw marks on an aspen trunk where a bear climbed. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GlacierNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bear_Claw_Marks_on_an_Aspen_Tree_(42501156050).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-bear-track-mud.jpg\" alt=\"Black bear track in mud. The hind foot looks a little like a human foot.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black bear track in mud. The hind foot looks a little like a human foot. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: ShenandoahNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Bear_Track_in_Mud_(44654784885).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Bear tracks show 5 toes and a wide pad; the hind track looks like a short, wide human foot <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Berry filled scat on a trail means a berry patch is close and in use (Tip).</li></ul><ul><li>Black bears den in mid October to late November in the Interior and come out in April and May <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Fresh sign, a carcass smell or ravens circling: back out, talk loudly, and keep bear spray ready (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): Find the groceries and you find the big animals. Willow for moose, wet meadows for elk, berries for bears.</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Calling every big track a moose. Check the size: elk tracks are smaller and rounder.</li><li>Walking into a berry patch with the wind at your back. The bear smells you; you do not see it.</li><li>Hunting dry timber for moose. They want water and willow.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Walk the edge of one wetland or beaver pond. Find browsed willow or dogwood, one big track and one scat. Measure the track and photograph everything with a ruler. Decide: moose, elk, deer or bear?</p></aside>","<ul><li>BC Ministry of Environment species accounts (2000 to 2001): <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/moose.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moose in BC</a>, <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/elk.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Elk in BC</a>, <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/blackbear.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Black Bears in BC</a>, checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>BC moose research progress report 2016 (Bonaparte Plateau study area), checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Habitat Conservation Trust Foundation, <a href=\"https://hctf.ca/return-of-the-roosevelts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Return of the Roosevelts</a>, checked 2026-10-03 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Gaines 2003, Canadian Field Naturalist, and WildSafeBC black bear page: den dates <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Elbroch, Mammal Tracks and Sign (Stackpole, 2003): track and pellet sizes, wallows, rubs, claw marks (75% to 80%).</li><li><a href=\"https://linnet.geog.ubc.ca/biodiversity/eflora/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">E Flora BC</a>: willow, dogwood, huckleberry, soopolallie (75% to 85%). Berry timing is local knowledge <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Photos: Wikimedia Commons, credits under each photo and on the Photo credits page. Checked 2026-10-04.</li></ul>"]